\pard\plain \fi720\sl288\slmult1\ltrch\loch {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Heyyyyy Grant,}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 There was an incident or two yesterday. I wanted to bring them to your attention, because it sort of typified a pattern of behavior I\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92ve been seeing in the dynamic between Caleb and his siblings. I feel compelled to point it out because it reminded me of my own unhappy childhood, and it sort of hurts to see it all playing out again. To be clear, I\u8217\'92m aware that I may be projecting myself and my own past experiences on what\u8217\'92s going on. I\u8217\'92m not around your kids every day, so you\u8217\'92ll have to use your own judgment to determine to what extent my own experiences are applicable.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 I grew up with a bully for an older brother. Not you, Jeff. Jeff was manipulative, physical, and lied constantly. Not to be too dramatic, but for some context, I first tried to kill myself when I was about Jackon\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s age. Mom was napping. Jeff was terrorizing me. I had no escape because if I woke mom up I would get in trouble, and so I snuck into the hallway closet and tried to hang myself with a belt. Jeff, still intent on messing with me, opened the closet door, saw what I was doing, then went and woke mom up. Mom sent us both to timeout in our beds. I felt victimized, punished for being punished. I don\u8217\'92t mean to paint myself as a little angel, but I do know what it\u8217\'92s like to have bully older brother.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Yesterday, Jackson, Caleb, and I were playing a scooter game in the neighbor\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s driveway. We were having fun. Jackson was a little frustrated because he wasn\u8217\'92t able to win, but, he was trying. At one point, he fell off his scooter. It wasn\u8217\'92t a hard fall, but he was already frustrated, and in that that moment where he was figuring out if he was hurt or not, Caleb runs up into his face and starts yelling and gloating. It wasn\u8217\'92t the meanest thing I\u8217\'92ve ever seen, but it was mean-spirited, and not nice. Jackson was clearly on the verge of tears, and for Caleb to see that, and then do what he did, well, it was mean. But Jackson didn\u8217\'92t just cry. He FREAKED out. He ran all the way over into our driveway, hid behind a car, plopped down and started bawling.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Jackon\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s behavior reminded me of me when I was kid; a pretty extreme emotional reaction, completely unable to regulate his nervous system in response to what was, in a vacuum, not *that* big of a deal.  What Caleb did wasn\u8217\'92t *that* bad. It was just plain mean.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 I tried to comfort Jackson, but I\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92m not very good at this. I didn\u8217\'92t handle it well. In retrospect I needed to be more direct and aggressive with the comfort I showed Jackson when Caleb bullied him. Next time. Anyways, Jackson ran inside to find grandma. Meanwhile, I had a talk with Caleb, tried to explain to him that he shouldn\u8217\'92t be mean to Jackson like that, that Jackson is his only brother, that life is already hard and family has to make it easier for each other, not more difficult. He sort of smiled, was embarrassed, knew what he did was wrong. Then Grandma came out of the house with Jackson in tow, and told Caleb to come with her.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Jackson picked up his scooter and together we continued to play the scooter game in the neighbor\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s driveway. At some point, Nora and Grandpa joined us. We were having lots of fun. All smiles. Then Caleb comes back out on his scooter. He scoots by Nora, and she screams, something to the effect of \u8220\'93Don\u8217\'92t grab me!\u8221\'94 She\u8217\'92d been totally chill just few seconds before. And Caleb didn\u8217\'92t even have any intent, he was just scooting by. His simple presence was enough to spike her anxiety into the atmosphere. Jackson\u8217\'92s too. And yours, honestly. Like, last night I saw you watch Caleb walking towards you in the pass-through in the kitchen. You seemed anxious. He bumped into you, uncomfortably. You didn\u8217\'92t like it. You, seemingly frustrated, sort of grabbed him awkwardly and tried to force him away. }
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 All this to say, Caleb is like an attention hungry bowling ball with little empathy. I\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92ve watched, over the years, as Jackson has subdued himself because any time he gets attention, here comes Caleb. And his emotional reactions have become more and more extreme because that\u8217\'92s the only way he gets any reprieve. And it\u8217\'92s got to be extreme because Caleb lies a LOT. Not with Jeff\u8217\'92s efficiency, but it\u8217\'92s a lot. When grandma took him inside and asked what happened in the driveway, he lied by saying Jackson started it by calling him a poopyhead. I was there, Jackson did not do ANYTHING to warrant retaliation. Kids lie, I know. People lie. But I think Caleb wouldn\u8217\'92t lie like this if it didn\u8217\'92t work. It certainly worked for Jeff.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 I don\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t know what I\u8217\'92m getting at here. I\u8217\'92m just concerned for Jackson, mainly. Remember, I\u8217\'92m viewing all this through the lens of having been a middle child with an older brother for a bully, so its *really* hard not to see myself in Jackson. It\u8217\'92s hard to remember that Jackson is not me, he is his own person, and Caleb is not Jeff. And I\u8217\'92m pretty sure you\u8217\'92re aware of the problem and might have a better handle on it already. All I want to say is this: Don\u8217\'92t let Jackson get lost in shuffle amidst all of Caleb\u8217\'92s bombastic behavior. And also, this will be difficult, but you reeeeeally got to figure out how and when Caleb is lying to avoid punishment, or otherwise get Jackson to share his punishment. Like, sometimes Jeff would be mean to me, I would then complain to mom, then Jeff would sell a lie to mom about me messing with him, and mom, not knowing who to believe, simply punished us both. Jeff would then feel real smug and pleased about the fact that I was being punished alongside him. It really made me feel like no one understood or heard me, and I internalized that, and had a very low sense of self worth that I\u8217\'92m still working through.\line }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 I think if you center Jackson\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s sense of self worth and build it up, even as Caleb tries to tear it down, Jackson won\u8217\'92t have as many difficulties when he gets older. So I\u8217\'92m not so much advocating for corrective measures towards Caleb, I don\u8217\'92t know how to parent; just show LOTS of love and encouragement to Jackson. Make him feel good about himself. I mean I know you\u8217\'92re his dad and I\u8217\'92m not doubting that you do that, I guess I\u8217\'92m just stressing the importance of it. With so many kids, it can get real easy for one or two to get lost in the shuffle, especially if a child subdues themself so that they don\u8217\'92t draw the attention of the bully.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
